---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Secure SPA Delivery with S3, CloudFront and OAC | Felipe Sales"
  description="Architecture decisions and validation steps for delivering a React SPA through CloudFront with a private S3 origin, managed TLS and Route 53."
>
  <article class="article-page">
    <a class="back-link" href={`${base}articles`}>← Back to articles</a>

    <p class="article-command">$ cat secure-spa-delivery-aws.md</p>
    <span class="article-category">AWS Architecture</span>

    <h1>Secure SPA Delivery with S3, CloudFront and OAC</h1>

    <p class="article-lead">
      How I published a React single-page application behind a custom domain
      while keeping the Amazon S3 origin private and preserving client-side routing.
    </p>

    <div class="article-meta">Published from a completed and validated AWS implementation.</div>

    <section>
      <h2>The challenge</h2>
      <p>
        A static frontend is simple to upload, but a production-minded delivery
        path has more responsibilities than object storage. The application needed
        HTTPS, a custom hostname, global delivery, protected origin access and
        support for direct navigation to routes handled by the React router.
      </p>
      <p>
        Making the S3 bucket public would have created a second entry point that
        bypassed CloudFront controls. Using the S3 website endpoint would also have
        prevented the origin from being protected with Origin Access Control.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <div class="architecture-flow" aria-label="Architecture request flow">
        <span>Browser</span><strong>→</strong><span>Route 53</span><strong>→</strong><span>CloudFront + ACM</span><strong>→</strong><span>Private S3</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Route 53 resolves the application hostname to CloudFront. The browser then
        establishes an HTTPS connection with the CDN. CloudFront serves cached
        objects when available and signs origin requests with OAC when it needs to
        read from the private S3 bucket.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Key technical decisions</h2>
      <div class="decision-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Private origin</h3>
          <p>S3 Block Public Access remains enabled. A distribution-scoped bucket policy authorizes CloudFront through OAC.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Managed TLS</h3>
          <p>ACM provides the certificate from us-east-1, the Region required for certificates attached to CloudFront.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Custom DNS</h3>
          <p>A Route 53 Alias record points the application hostname to the CloudFront distribution.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>SPA routing</h3>
          <p>Custom 403 and 404 responses return index.html with status 200 so the client-side router can handle direct navigation.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>HTTPS only</h3>
          <p>The viewer protocol policy redirects HTTP requests to HTTPS before the application content is delivered.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Cache-aware deployment</h3>
          <p>Publishing includes CloudFront invalidation considerations; uploading new files to S3 alone does not guarantee that users receive the latest index.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Validation evidence</h2>
      <p>
        I treated deployment as incomplete until the public path and the security
        boundary had both been checked. The observed results were:
      </p>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li><strong>HTTP to HTTPS:</strong> the HTTP endpoint redirected to the HTTPS application URL.</li>
        <li><strong>Main page:</strong> the application returned a successful HTTPS response.</li>
        <li><strong>SPA fallback:</strong> an unknown route returned index.html with status 200.</li>
        <li><strong>Private origin:</strong> direct S3 access returned 403 AccessDenied.</li>
        <li><strong>Edge cache:</strong> repeated requests produced a CloudFront cache hit.</li>
        <li><strong>S3 controls:</strong> Block Public Access remained enabled and ACLs were disabled.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this project reinforced</h2>
      <p>
        DNS resolves the hostname but does not proxy the application. CloudFront is
        the public delivery boundary, while S3 remains a regional private origin.
        Keeping one public entry point makes TLS, caching and access control easier
        to reason about and validate.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also reinforced that a deployment procedure must include routing and
        cache checks. A successful upload is only one step; the delivered behavior
        is what needs to be tested.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="article-actions">
      <a href="https://residencia.fsocloudconsulting.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open live application →</a>
      <a href="https://felipesalesdeoliveira.github.io/fso-cloud-consulting/en/projects/frontend-cdn-s3-tls-custom-domain/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View full technical case →</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/felipesalesdeoliveira/residencia-trilha-foundation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View repository →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-page {
    max-width: 900px;
    min-height: 100vh;
    padding: 130px 0 100px;
  }

  .back-link,
  .article-actions a {
    color: #36d399;
    font-size: 13px;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .article-command {
    margin: 42px 0 16px;
    color: #36d399;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .article-category {
    color: #5ca8ff;
    font-size: 11px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.8px;
  }

  h1 {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    color: #e7ebec;
    font-family: Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(38px, 6vw, 62px);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -2px;
  }

  .article-lead {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 26px 0 0;
    color: #aeb5b7;
    font-family: Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .article-meta {
    margin-top: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 34px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #252d30;
    color: #667174;
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  section {
    margin-top: 54px;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    color: #dfe4e5;
    font-family: Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    font-size: 28px;
  }

  p,
  .checks {
    color: #9ba4a7;
    font-family: Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .architecture-flow {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 28px 0;
    padding: 24px;
    border: 1px solid #283235;
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: #0d1214;
  }

  .architecture-flow span {
    padding: 10px 13px;
    border: 1px solid #303a3d;
    border-radius: 7px;
    color: #d9ddde;
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  .architecture-flow strong {
    color: #36d399;
  }

  .decision-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .decision-grid article {
    padding: 22px;
    border: 1px solid #283235;
    border-radius: 9px;
    background: #0d1214;
  }

  .decision-grid h3 {
    margin: 0;
    color: #d9ddde;
    font-family: Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    font-size: 17px;
  }

  .decision-grid p {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .checks {
    padding-left: 22px;
  }

  .checks li {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .checks strong {
    color: #d9ddde;
  }

  .article-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 18px;
    margin-top: 60px;
    padding-top: 30px;
    border-top: 1px solid #252d30;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .article-page {
      padding-top: 110px;
    }

    .decision-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .article-lead {
      font-size: 18px;
    }
  }
</style>
